from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('topic-07','networks','network-theorems','Network Theorems · Equivalents, Sources & Power','003');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'In the shown divider, the load at a,b is open. Find the Thevenin voltage $V_{ab}$ in volts.',8,'12 V ideal source; upper resistance 2 kΩ; lower resistance 4 kΩ; open output','Find the open-circuit port voltage.','Thevenin voltage',r'V_{th}=V_s\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}',r'No load current is drawn, so the 2 kΩ and 4 kΩ resistors form a series divider. | The source current is 12/(2+4)=2 mA. | The voltage at a relative to b is (2 mA)(4 kΩ)=8 V.','Do not short the output when finding the open-circuit voltage.',difficulty='easy',unit='V',diagram=('assets/diagrams/thevenin-divider.svg','12 V source drives 2 kΩ to port a; 4 kΩ connects a to common b; output open.'))
b.n(r'For the same explicitly defined network, a 12 V source drives 2 kΩ to port a, and 4 kΩ connects a to ground b. Find the Thevenin resistance seen at a,b in kΩ.',1.3333333333333333,'Independent ideal voltage source; R₁=2 kΩ; R₂=4 kΩ','Find the deactivated-source port resistance.','Source suppression',r'R_{th}=R_1\parallel R_2',r'Deactivate the independent voltage source by replacing it with a short circuit. | Both resistors then connect between a and b in parallel. | Rth=(2×4)/(2+4)=4/3 kΩ.','An ideal voltage source becomes a short, not an open, when suppressed.',unit='kΩ')
b.n(r'A Thevenin equivalent has $V_{th}=18$ V and $R_{th}=6$ Ω. Find its Norton current in amperes.',3,'Vth=18 V; Rth=6 Ω','Find the equivalent short-circuit current.','Thevenin–Norton conversion',r'I_N=V_{th}/R_{th}',r'The Norton current equals the short-circuit port current. | Shorting a 6 Ω Thevenin source gives current 18/6. | Hence IN=3 A, with the equivalent resistance unchanged at 6 Ω.','The Norton resistance equals Rth; it does not become zero.',difficulty='easy',unit='A')
b.m(r'When applying superposition to a linear circuit containing an independent current source, how is that source suppressed?','C',['Replace it with a short','Replace it with its current value as resistance','Replace it with an open','Delete all resistors in its branch'],'Ideal independent current source; linear circuit','Choose the zero-source replacement.','Superposition source suppression',r'I_s=0\ \Rightarrow\ \text{open circuit}',r'Superposition sets all but one independent source to zero. | Zero current through an ideal current-source branch means the branch is open. | Replace the suppressed independent current source with an open circuit; dependent sources remain active.','An ideal current source is suppressed differently from an ideal voltage source.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A load resistor is connected to a 20 V Thevenin source with 5 Ω series resistance. What maximum load power can be obtained in watts?',20,'Vth=20 V; Rth=5 Ω; positive resistive load','Find the largest load power.','Maximum power transfer',r'P_L=\frac{V_{th}^2R_L}{(R_{th}+R_L)^2}',r'Differentiating with respect to RL gives a maximum at RL=Rth=5 Ω. | At this load the current is 20/(5+5)=2 A. | The load power is I²RL=4×5=20 W.','The condition maximizes power, not source-to-load efficiency.',unit='W')
b.s(r'For a linear network used in Thevenin-resistance determination, which statements are correct?',['A','C'],['Independent voltage sources are set to zero voltage','Dependent sources are always removed','A test source may be needed when dependent sources are present','All current sources, including dependent ones, must be opened'],'Linear networks may contain controlled sources','Select valid resistance-computation rules.','Test-source method',r'R_{th}=V_{test}/I_{test}',r'Only independent sources are suppressed; a zero-voltage source becomes a short. | Dependent-source constitutive relations remain in force. A test source measures the resulting port V/I when needed. | Thus A and C hold. Removing a controlled source changes the original network.','Do not suppress a source merely because it is drawn as a source symbol.')
b.n(r'A port has a 2 kΩ resistor in parallel with a dependent current source $i_d=0.25v$ mA, directed from the port to ground when port voltage v is in volts. Find the input resistance in kΩ.',1.3333333333333333,'Port shunt resistor 2 kΩ; dependent sink current 0.25v mA','Find Vtest/Itest.','Dependent-source port resistance',r'I_{test}=V_{test}/R+gV_{test}',r'Apply a 1 V test source, with current defined entering the network. | The resistor draws 0.5 mA and the dependent source draws 0.25 mA, giving total 0.75 mA. | Rin=1 V/0.75 mA=4/3 kΩ.','The direction of the controlled current determines whether its conductance adds or subtracts.',difficulty='hard',unit='kΩ')
b.n(r'A Norton source of 6 A feeds a 3 Ω internal shunt resistor and a 6 Ω load. Find the load current in amperes.',2,'IN=6 A; internal resistance 3 Ω; load 6 Ω','Find load-branch current.','Norton current division',r'I_L=I_N\frac{R_N}{R_N+R_L}',r'The two resistors share a port voltage and divide source current inversely with resistance. | IL=6×3/(3+6). | This is 2 A; the remaining 4 A flows in the 3 Ω branch, satisfying KCL.','The resistance in the current-divider numerator is the other branch resistance.',unit='A')
b.n(r'With two independent sources in a linear circuit, their separate contributions to a branch current are 2 A and −0.5 A in the same reference direction. Find the total current in amperes.',1.5,'Source contributions 2 A and −0.5 A','Sum branch-current responses with sign.','Superposition of responses',r'i=i_1+i_2',r'Linearity permits addition of signed current responses. | Use the specified common reference direction: 2+(−0.5). | The total is 1.5 A in the positive direction.','Source contributions are signed; subtracting a negative value again changes the physics.',difficulty='easy',unit='A')
b.m(r'A sinusoidal Thevenin source has internal impedance $Z_{th}=3+j4$ Ω. Which unconstrained complex load impedance maximizes average load power?','B',[r'$3+j4$ Ω',r'$3-j4$ Ω',r'$5$ Ω',r'$-3+j4$ Ω'],'Positive source resistance; reactive load matching allowed','Choose the conjugately matched load.','AC maximum power transfer',r'Z_L=Z_{th}^*',r'Average load power depends on the real load resistance and total impedance magnitude. | Choosing load reactance −4 Ω cancels the source reactance; resistance 3 Ω then matches the source resistance. | The optimum is ZL=3−j4 Ω, not a negative-resistance load.','Complex conjugation changes only the sign of the imaginary component.')
b.n(r'A 10 V Thevenin source with 2 Ω resistance drives an 8 Ω load. Find the efficiency $100P_L/P_{source}$ in percent.',80,'Vth=10 V; series Rth=2 Ω; RL=8 Ω; ideal DC source','Find the source-to-load efficiency.','Power partition in an equivalent source',r'\eta=\frac{R_L}{R_{th}+R_L}',r'The source current is 10/(2+8)=1 A. | The load absorbs 8 W and the ideal source supplies 10 W. | Efficiency is 100×8/10=80%. Maximum-power matching would instead give 50% here.','Maximum-power transfer and maximum efficiency are different design targets.')
b.s(r'In a passive linear bilateral network containing only resistors, a 1 A test current into port 1 produces 2 V at open port 2. Which conclusions follow from reciprocity?',['A','D'],['A 1 A test current into port 2 produces 2 V at open port 1','The driving-point resistance of port 1 must be 2 Ω','The two driving-point resistances must be identical','The open-circuit transfer resistances z12 and z21 are equal'],'Linear bilateral resistive two-port; other port open in each test','Apply reciprocity to transfer resistance.','Reciprocity',r'z_{12}=z_{21}',r'The given experiment specifies z21=V2/I1=2 Ω with I2=0. | Reciprocity makes z12 also 2 Ω, so reversing the current/voltage ports gives 2 V. | It imposes equality of transfer parameters, not equality of the driving-point parameters z11 and z22. Select A and D.','Do not replace a transfer resistance by a driving-point resistance.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-08','networks','transient-analysis','Transients · Initial Conditions, Poles & Energy','003');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'An initially uncharged 2 μF capacitor is charged by a 10 V step through 5 kΩ. Find the time constant in milliseconds.',10,'R=5 kΩ; C=2 μF; zero initial voltage','Find RC in ms.','RC time constant',r'\tau=RC',r'Convert values: R=5000 Ω and C=2×10⁻⁶ F. | Their product is 0.01 s. | Multiplying by 1000 gives a time constant of 10 ms.','Keep kilo and micro prefixes consistent.',difficulty='easy',unit='ms')
b.n(r'A capacitor initially at 8 V discharges through a resistor with RC=2 s. Find its voltage at t=2 ln2 seconds.',4,'Initial voltage 8 V; natural time constant 2 s','Find the discharging voltage.','First-order natural response',r'v(t)=V_0e^{-t/\tau}',r'The circuit has no sustaining source, so its final voltage is zero. | At t=2 ln2, t/τ=ln2 and the exponential factor is 1/2. | The voltage is 8/2=4 V.','For natural discharge, the exponential multiplies the initial voltage.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An initially unenergized 3 H inductor is connected to 12 V DC through 6 Ω. Find its steady-state current in amperes.',2,'L=3 H; R=6 Ω; source 12 V; t→∞','Find final inductor current.','DC RL steady state',r'i(\infty)=V/R',r'At DC steady state, an ideal inductor has zero voltage. | The entire 12 V appears across the 6 Ω resistor. | The final current is 12/6=2 A, independent of L.','The inductor controls the transient speed, not the final DC current.',difficulty='easy',unit='A')
b.n(r'For a source-free series RL circuit with R=4 Ω and L=2 H, enter the natural pole in s⁻¹.',-2,'Series source-free RL; R=4 Ω; L=2 H','Find the exponential pole.','RL natural pole',r'L\frac{di}{dt}+Ri=0,\quad p=-R/L',r'KVL gives 2 di/dt+4i=0. | Writing di/dt=−2i identifies the exponent coefficient. | The pole is −2 s⁻¹ and the time constant is 0.5 s.','A passive source-free RL circuit has a negative real pole.',unit='s⁻¹')
b.m(r'A finite current is applied to an ideal capacitor at switching. Which quantity must remain continuous across that instant?','B',['Capacitor current','Capacitor voltage','Resistor voltage in every circuit','Time derivative of capacitor voltage'],'Capacitor driven without an impulse of current','Identify the continuous state variable.','Capacitor state continuity',r'\Delta v_C=\frac1C\int_{0^-}^{0^+}i_C(t)dt',r'The integral of bounded current over a vanishing interval is zero. | Therefore the capacitor voltage cannot change instantaneously under the stated condition. | Current or voltage derivative may jump, so only B is guaranteed.','An impulse current would invalidate the bounded-current condition.')
b.n(r'An ideal 1 F capacitor has v(0+)=2 V. It is connected through 2 Ω to a 10 V source. Find dv/dt at 0+ in V/s.',4,'C=1 F; v(0+)=2 V; source 10 V; R=2 Ω','Find initial voltage slope.','Initial-value capacitor KCL',r'C\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{V_s-v}{R}',r'The initial resistor voltage is 10−2=8 V. | Its current into the capacitor is 8/2=4 A. | Dividing by C=1 F gives initial slope 4 V/s.','Use the capacitor’s initial voltage rather than assuming it is uncharged.',unit='V/s')
b.n(r'For a series RLC circuit with R=4 Ω, L=1 H and C=1 F, find the damping ratio ζ.',2,'Series RLC; R=4 Ω; L=1 H; C=1 F','Find ζ in the standard second-order denominator.','Second-order damping',r'\omega_n=1/\sqrt{LC},\quad\zeta=\frac{R}{2}\sqrt{C/L}',r'The characteristic equation is s²+(R/L)s+1/(LC)=s²+4s+1. | Match with s²+2ζωn s+ωn²; ωn=1 rad/s. | Then 2ζ=4, so ζ=2 and the response is overdamped.','The damping ratio is dimensionless; α and ζ differ by ωn.')
b.s(r'Which statements hold for source-free passive ideal RLC networks with positive R,L,C and no impulses?',['A','C','D'],['Stored energy cannot increase because of resistive dissipation','An ideal inductor current can jump under bounded voltage','Capacitor voltage is continuous under bounded current','An underdamped second-order response has complex-conjugate poles'],'Passive source-free network; bounded switch waveforms','Select valid energy and continuity statements.','Energy and natural modes',r'W=\tfrac12Cv^2+\tfrac12Li^2,\quad\frac{dW}{dt}=-P_R',r'With no source, resistor power removes stored energy, so dW/dt≤0. | Integrating the inductor voltage law under bounded voltage makes current continuous, so B is false. | Capacitor voltage is likewise continuous; underdamping corresponds to conjugate poles. Select A, C and D.','A source-free system can exchange energy between L and C without increasing total stored energy.')
b.n(r'A first-order RC step response starts at 0 V and approaches 5 V with τ=1 s. Find the time to first reach 4 V, in seconds.',1.6094379124341003,'v(0)=0; v∞=5 V; τ=1 s; target 4 V','Find the crossing time.','Settling to a specified fraction',r'v(t)=V_\infty(1-e^{-t/\tau})',r'Set 4=5(1−e⁻ᵗ). | Rearrange to e⁻ᵗ=1/5. | Taking natural logarithms gives t=ln5≈1.609437912 s.','Use the remaining error fraction 1/5 rather than the reached fraction 4/5.',unit='s')
b.n(r'A 2 F capacitor changes from 3 V to 1 V during passive discharge. Find the energy released in joules.',8,'C=2 F; initial voltage 3 V; final voltage 1 V','Find the decrease in stored energy.','Capacitor energy difference',r'\Delta W=\tfrac12 C(V_i^2-V_f^2)',r'Initial stored energy is ½×2×9=9 J. | Final stored energy is ½×2×1=1 J. | The capacitor releases 9−1=8 J.','Square each voltage before taking their difference.',unit='J')
b.n(r'The response $v(t)=e^{-t}-e^{-2t}$ for t≥0 reaches its maximum at t = ln2. Find the maximum value.',0.25,'Difference of two decaying modes; specified extremum time','Find the peak value and verify it is a maximum.','Two-mode transient peak',r'v\prime(t)=-e^{-t}+2e^{-2t}',r'The derivative is zero when eᵗ=2, at t=ln2. | There e⁻ᵗ=1/2 and e⁻²ᵗ=1/4. | The peak is 1/2−1/4=1/4; the second derivative there is −1/2<0.','A sum of decaying terms need not decay monotonically if coefficients have opposite signs.',difficulty='hard')
b.m(r'A series RLC circuit has characteristic polynomial $s^2+6s+9$. Its natural response is:','C',['Underdamped','Undamped','Critically damped','Unstable'],'Characteristic polynomial s²+6s+9','Classify the damping.','Repeated real natural poles',r's^2+6s+9=(s+3)^2',r'The polynomial has a repeated pole at −3. | Comparing with s²+2ζωn s+ωn² gives ωn=3 and ζ=1. | A stable repeated second-order pole at ζ=1 is the critically damped case.','A repeated negative pole is not an oscillatory complex pair.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-10','networks','fourier-transform','Fourier Analysis · Spectra, Symmetry & Parseval','003');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Use $X(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x(t)e^{-j\omega t}dt$. For x(t)=1 on −1≤t≤1 and zero elsewhere, find X(0).',2,'Unit rectangular pulse of duration 2; angular-frequency Fourier convention','Find the DC transform value.','Fourier transform at zero',r'X(0)=\int x(t)dt',r'At ω=0 the complex exponential equals 1. | The transform is therefore the pulse area. | Height 1 times width 2 gives X(0)=2.','X(0) is area, not the signal value x(0).',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ and the same angular-frequency Fourier convention, find $|X(0)|$.',0.5,'Causal real exponential with rate 2','Find transform magnitude at zero.','Exponential Fourier transform',r'X(\omega)=\frac1{2+j\omega}',r'Integrate e⁻⁽²⁺ʲω⁾ᵗ over t≥0. | The integral equals 1/(2+jω). | At zero frequency this is real 1/2, whose magnitude is 0.5.','The pole location and the DC magnitude are reciprocal quantities.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'If y(t)=x(t−3) and x has transform X(ω), which transform is correct?','A',[r'$e^{-j3\omega}X(\omega)$',r'$e^{j3\omega}X(\omega)$',r'$X(\omega-3)$',r'$3X(\omega)$'],'Time delay 3 units; standard angular-frequency convention','Find the delayed transform.','Time-shift property',r'x(t-t_0)\longleftrightarrow e^{-j\omega t_0}X(\omega)',r'Substitute τ=t−3 in the Fourier integral. | The exponential splits into e⁻ʲωτ times e⁻ʲ³ω. | The remaining integral is X(ω), so A is correct.','A delay adds negative linear phase and does not shift the spectrum in frequency.')
b.n(r'The signal $x(t)=3+2\cos(4t)$ is periodic. Find its average power over a period.',11,'DC level 3; sinusoidal amplitude 2; nonzero frequency','Find period-averaged squared amplitude.','Power of orthogonal Fourier components',r'P=|a_0|^2+\frac{A^2}{2}',r'Squaring gives 9+12cos(4t)+4cos²(4t). | Over one period, the cosine averages to 0 and cos² averages to 1/2. | The power is 9+4/2=11.','Do not add sinusoidal amplitudes and square the resulting number.')
b.n(r'For $x(t)=\cos(6\pi t)$, find the fundamental frequency in hertz.',3,'Angular frequency 6π rad/s','Convert angular frequency to cycles per second.','Frequency units',r'f_0=\omega_0/(2\pi)',r'One cycle corresponds to 2π radians. | Divide 6π rad/s by 2π rad/cycle. | The fundamental frequency is 3 Hz.','Do not treat rad/s and Hz as identical units.',difficulty='easy',unit='Hz')
b.s(r'For a real, even, absolutely integrable continuous-time signal x(t), which properties hold for its Fourier transform X(ω)?',['A','C','D'],['X(ω) is real','X(ω) is necessarily nonnegative','X(ω) is even','X(−ω) = X*(ω)'],'Real even integrable signal; standard Fourier transform','Select all necessary transform symmetries.','Fourier symmetry',r'X(\omega)=\int x(t)\cos\omega t\,dt-j\int x(t)\sin\omega t\,dt',r'The sine integrand is odd and integrates to zero, so X is real. | Cosine is even in ω, so X is even; reality of x also yields conjugate symmetry. | The rectangular pulse has a sinc transform with negative lobes, disproving nonnegativity. Select A, C and D.','Real-valued spectrum does not mean nonnegative spectrum.')
b.n(r'If x(t) has energy E=6, find the energy of y(t)=x(3t).',2,'Finite energy 6; time scaling factor 3','Find the scaled energy.','Time scaling of energy',r'E_y=\int|x(at)|^2dt=E_x/|a|',r'Substitute τ=3t, so dt=dτ/3. | The integral becomes one third of the original energy. | Hence Ey=6/3=2.','Compressing time decreases the duration and therefore the energy at fixed amplitude.')
b.n(r'For $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$, find $\int |X(\omega)|^2d\omega/(2\pi)$ under the standard angular-frequency convention.',0.5,'Causal exponential of unit rate; Parseval normalization 1/(2π)','Find the frequency-domain energy.','Parseval identity',r'\frac1{2\pi}\int|X(\omega)|^2d\omega=\int|x(t)|^2dt',r'The time-domain squared magnitude is e⁻²ᵗ for t≥0. | Its integral is [−e⁻²ᵗ/2] from 0 to infinity. | This gives 1/2, equal to the normalized spectral energy.','The 1/(2π) factor depends on the Fourier convention and is given explicitly.')
b.m(r'For $y(t)=e^{j5t}x(t)$, which relation holds?','D',[r'$Y(\omega)=X(5\omega)$',r'$Y(\omega)=e^{-j5\omega}X(\omega)$',r'$Y(\omega)=X(\omega+5)$',r'$Y(\omega)=X(\omega-5)$'],'Complex modulation by eʲ⁵ᵗ','Find the shifted spectrum.','Frequency translation',r'e^{j\omega_ct}x(t)\longleftrightarrow X(\omega-\omega_c)',r'Combine the modulation exponential with the Fourier kernel. | eʲ⁵ᵗe⁻ʲωᵗ=e⁻ʲ⁽ω⁻⁵⁾ᵗ. | The original transform is evaluated at ω−5, giving D.','Modulation shifts frequency; delay multiplies by a phase factor.')
b.n(r'A periodic signal has complex Fourier-series coefficients c0=1, c1=c−1=2, and all others zero. Find its average power.',9,'Complex coefficients 1,2,2; remaining coefficients zero','Find power using series coefficients.','Fourier-series Parseval theorem',r'P=\sum_k|c_k|^2',r'Complex exponentials are orthogonal over the fundamental period. | Square and sum the three nonzero coefficient magnitudes. | Power is 1²+2²+2²=9.','Both positive and negative frequency coefficients contribute.')
b.s(r'Assume x and y and the relevant transforms exist. Which statements hold under $X(\omega)=\int x(t)e^{-j\omega t}dt$?',['A','B','D'],['Time convolution transforms to X(ω)Y(ω)','Time reversal x(−t) transforms to X(−ω)','Time multiplication transforms to X(ω)Y(ω) without convolution','A real odd integrable signal has a purely imaginary transform'],'Standard Fourier convention; integrable signals where required','Select correct transform properties.','Convolution and parity',r'x*y\longleftrightarrow XY,\quad xy\longleftrightarrow\frac1{2\pi}(X*Y)',r'Substitution in the convolution integral separates it into a product of transforms. | Reversing the time variable reverses the frequency variable. | Multiplication requires spectral convolution; for a real odd signal the cosine part vanishes and only the imaginary sine part remains. Select A, B and D.','Multiplication and convolution are dual operations and must not be interchanged.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'For the even pulse x(t)=1 when |t|≤1 and zero otherwise, find the smallest positive zero of X(ω), in rad/s.',3.141592653589793,'Centered width-2 pulse; angular-frequency transform','Find the first strictly positive spectral zero.','Rectangular-pulse transform zeros',r'X(\omega)=2\sin\omega/\omega',r'Integrating the even pulse gives 2sin(ω)/ω. | Nonzero roots occur at integer multiples of π. | At ω=0 the removable limit is 2, so the first positive zero is π rad/s.','The zero at the numerator’s origin cancels with the denominator; it is not a spectral null.',difficulty='hard',unit='rad/s')

b=Bank('topic-12','networks','lti-systems','LTI Systems · Convolution, Stability & Delay','003');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and input $x(t)=u(t)$, find y(ln2).',0.5,'Causal impulse response e⁻ᵗ; unit-step input; zero initial state','Find the step-response value.','Convolution with a unit step',r'y(t)=\int_0^t h(\tau)d\tau',r'For t≥0 the step selects impulse-response contributions from 0 to t. | Integrating e⁻τ gives y(t)=1−e⁻ᵗ. | At t=ln2 the output is 1−1/2=0.5.','Impulse response and step response are different functions.')
b.n(r'For sequences x[0]=1, x[1]=2 and h[0]=1, h[1]=−1, with all other samples zero, find (x*h)[1].',1,'Two length-2 sequences; zero elsewhere','Find the n=1 convolution sample.','Discrete convolution',r'y[n]=\sum_k x[k]h[n-k]',r'At n=1, only k=0 and k=1 contribute. | y[1]=x[0]h[1]+x[1]h[0]=1(−1)+2(1). | Therefore y[1]=1.','Convolution reverses one index; it is not pointwise multiplication.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'Which continuous-time LTI impulse response is causal and BIBO stable?','B',[r'$e^t u(t)$',r'$e^{-2t}u(t)$',r'$u(t)$',r'$e^{-t}u(-t)$'],'Candidate impulse responses; standard causality and BIBO definitions','Choose a causal stable response.','LTI stability test',r'h(t)=0\ (t<0),\quad\int|h(t)|dt<\infty',r'B is zero for negative time and has finite absolute integral 1/2. | A grows, C has infinite area, and D is noncausal and grows toward negative infinity. | Only B satisfies both required properties.','Causality does not imply stability.')
b.s(r'Which systems are linear and time invariant?',['A','D'],['y(t)=2x(t)','y(t)=x²(t)','y(t)=t x(t)','y(t)=x(t−2)'],'Scalar signal-to-signal operators','Select LTI mappings.','Superposition and time invariance',r'T[ax_1+bx_2]=aT[x_1]+bT[x_2]',r'A constant gain obeys superposition and commutes with time shifts. | Squaring violates linearity; multiplying by absolute time is linear but time varying. | A fixed delay commutes with shifts and is linear. Select A and D.','Linearity and time invariance are separate tests.')
b.n(r'A causal LTI system has $H(s)=1/(s+4)$. Find its DC gain.',0.25,'Causal stable first-order transfer function','Find H(0).','DC frequency response',r'H(0)=\left.H(s)\right|_{s=0}',r'The pole −4 lies in the left half-plane. | For a constant input, the steady gain is the transfer function at s=0. | H(0)=1/4=0.25.','The gain is not the pole magnitude 4.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'The impulse response is $h(t)=2\delta(t)+3\delta(t-1)$. For x(t)=1 at all real t, find the output amplitude.',5,'Two weighted impulses; input identically 1','Find the constant output.','Impulse sifting in convolution',r'y(t)=2x(t)+3x(t-1)',r'Convolution with δ(t) returns the original signal and with δ(t−1) returns a delayed signal. | A constant signal remains 1 under a delay. | Thus y=2×1+3×1=5.','The delayed impulse does not disappear for an input defined at every time.')
b.n(r'For a pure delay system $H(j\omega)=e^{-j2\omega}$, find the group delay in seconds.',2,'Unwrapped phase −2ω radians','Find −dφ/dω.','Group delay',r'\tau_g=-\frac{d\phi}{d\omega}',r'The phase is linear in angular frequency: φ=−2ω. | Differentiate to obtain dφ/dω=−2. | Negating yields group delay 2 s at every frequency.','Differentiate an unwrapped phase, not the discontinuous principal phase.',unit='s')
b.n(r'Two causal first-order systems with h1(t)=e⁻ᵗu(t) and h2(t)=e⁻ᵗu(t) are cascaded. Find the overall impulse response at t=1.',0.36787944117144233,'Identical causal exponential impulse responses; cascade; zero initial state','Find the convolution at t=1.','Cascade convolution',r'h(t)=\int_0^t e^{-\tau}e^{-(t-\tau)}d\tau',r'The product inside the integration simplifies to e⁻ᵗ, independent of τ. | Integrating over length t gives h(t)=te⁻ᵗu(t). | At t=1 this is e⁻¹≈0.3678794412.','Cascade impulse responses convolve; they do not simply multiply in time.')
b.s(r'A causal continuous-time rational LTI transfer function has no pole-zero cancellations. Which pole sets imply BIBO stability?',['A','D'],['{−1, −2}','{0, −2}','{+1, −3}','{−1+j, −1−j}'],'Causality; no hidden cancellations','Select stable pole sets.','Causal rational stability',r'\operatorname{Re}(p_i)<0\quad\text{for every pole}',r'For a causal rational response the ROC lies to the right of its rightmost pole. | BIBO stability requires the imaginary axis in the ROC, which occurs only when all poles are strictly left of it. | A and D qualify; the pole at zero in B and right-half-plane pole in C do not.','A pole on the imaginary axis fails the strict BIBO condition.')
b.n(r'For h[n]=(1/2)ⁿu[n] and x[n]=δ[n]+δ[n−1], find y[2].',0.75,'Causal geometric impulse response; two unit impulses at n=0,1','Find the output at n=2.','Shifted discrete impulses',r'y[n]=h[n]+h[n-1]',r'Linearity and time invariance map each shifted impulse to the correspondingly shifted h. | At n=2, h[2]=1/4 and h[1]=1/2. | Their sum is 3/4.','The delayed impulse adds h[n−1], not h[n+1].')
b.n(r'An LTI system has h(t)=u(t)−u(t−2). For x(t)=u(t)−u(t−3), find y(2.5).',2,'Unit rectangles on [0,2) and [0,3)','Find convolution overlap length at t=2.5.','Overlap geometry in convolution',r'y(t)=\text{length}([0,3]\cap[t-2,t])',r'The input-support variable τ lies in [0,3]. | The impulse term is nonzero when τ lies in [t−2,t]=[0.5,2.5]. | This interval is fully inside [0,3], so its length and the convolution value are 2.','Convolution uses a reflected and shifted interval, not the unchanged original overlap.',difficulty='hard')
b.m(r'An LTI system has impulse response h(t)=δ(t+1). Which description is correct?','C',['Causal unit delay','Unstable time-varying system','BIBO-stable noncausal unit advance','Memoryless gain 1'],'Impulse at t=−1','Classify the shift system.','Advance, causality and stability',r'y(t)=x(t+1)',r'Sifting gives output x(t+1), so the system needs a future input sample. | It is noncausal and has memory, but any bounded input stays bounded with the same bound. | Therefore it is BIBO-stable, LTI, and a one-unit advance: C.','A noncausal system can still be BIBO-stable.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-13','networks','sampling','Sampling · Reconstruction & Aliasing','003');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A real baseband signal has spectral support |f|≤3 kHz. Find its Nyquist rate in samples/s.',6000,'Highest spectral frequency 3000 Hz','Find twice the highest frequency.','Nyquist rate',r'f_N=2f_{max}',r'The Nyquist rate is twice the upper baseband frequency. | 3 kHz is 3000 Hz. | The rate is 2×3000=6000 samples/s; practical ideal-reconstruction sampling is taken strictly above it to avoid endpoint ambiguity.','A Nyquist rate is a boundary; sampling exactly there can lose a component with an unfortunate phase.',difficulty='easy',unit='samples/s')
b.n(r'A 900 Hz cosine is sampled at fs=1000 samples/s. Find its aliased frequency in [0,fs/2], in hertz.',100,'Input cosine 900 Hz; sample rate 1000 Hz','Find the baseband alias.','Spectral folding',r'f_a=|f-kf_s|\quad\text{folded into }[0,f_s/2]',r'Subtract one sampling frequency: 900−1000=−100 Hz. | For a real cosine, negative and positive frequencies represent the same cosine samples. | The baseband alias is 100 Hz.','The alias must lie within the first Nyquist interval.',unit='Hz')
b.m(r'For ideal reconstruction of every baseband signal with |f|≤4 kHz, which proposed rate safely satisfies the strict sampling condition?','D',['4 ksample/s','6 ksample/s','8 ksample/s','10 ksample/s'],'Arbitrary signals up to and including 4 kHz','Choose a rate strictly greater than twice the bandwidth.','Strict anti-alias sampling condition',r'f_s>2B',r'Twice the maximum frequency is 8 ksample/s. | Sampling exactly at 8 can fail for a sinusoid at the endpoint with suitable phase. | Of the options, only 10 ksample/s is strictly greater than 8.','Do not regard a boundary rate as safe for every possible endpoint phase.')
b.n(r'A signal is sampled every 0.25 ms. Find the sample rate in hertz.',4000,'Sampling period Ts=0.25 ms','Find fs in Hz.','Sampling-period conversion',r'f_s=1/T_s',r'Convert 0.25 ms to 0.00025 s. | Take the reciprocal. | The rate is 1/0.00025=4000 Hz.','Convert milliseconds to seconds before taking the reciprocal.',difficulty='easy',unit='Hz')
b.s(r'Which actions can help avoid irreversible aliasing when sampling an unknown analog baseband input?',['A','C'],['Low-pass filter the analog signal before sampling','Apply a digital low-pass filter only after aliasing has already occurred','Increase the sample rate above twice the passed analog bandwidth','Keep any sample rate and recover lost frequencies by zero padding'],'Unknown baseband analog signal; no prior sparse-frequency model','Select measures that prevent aliasing.','Analog anti-alias protection',r'\operatorname{supp}X(f)\subset(-f_s/2,f_s/2)',r'Analog low-pass filtering can remove energy that would overlap spectral replicas. | A sufficiently high sampling rate separates those replicas. | Digital filtering or zero padding cannot generally reverse overlap that already merged unknown analog components. Select A and C.','Prevention before sampling is different from smoothing aliased samples later.')
b.n(r'The sampled sequence is x[n]=cos(πn/3), with fs=1200 Hz. Find the smallest nonnegative analog cosine frequency that produces it.',200,'Discrete angular frequency π/3 rad/sample; fs=1200 Hz','Find the baseband analog frequency.','Discrete and continuous frequency mapping',r'\Omega=2\pi f/f_s',r'Rearrange the frequency relation to f=Ωfs/(2π). | Substitute Ω=π/3 and fs=1200. | The smallest nonnegative solution is 1200/6=200 Hz; higher aliases also produce the same sequence.','Rad/sample is not rad/second.',unit='Hz')
b.n(r'A finite observation lasts 0.1 seconds and contains exactly 500 uniformly spaced samples on the half-open interval [0,0.1). Find fs in samples/s.',5000,'500 samples; duration 0.1 s; endpoint excluded','Find the sample rate.','Uniform sample counting',r'N=f_sT',r'The half-open interval contains n=0 through N−1 without a duplicate endpoint. | Therefore fs=N/T. | fs=500/0.1=5000 samples/s.','Including both endpoints would change the sample-count convention.',unit='samples/s')
b.n(r'A 300 Hz cosine is sampled initially at 2000 samples/s. Keeping every fourth sample without filtering produces a new sequence. Find its folded baseband frequency in hertz, measured at the new sample rate.',200,'Original tone 300 Hz; original fs=2000 Hz; decimation factor 4; no prefilter','Find the alias after decimation.','Sample-rate reduction',r'f_{s,new}=f_s/M',r'Keeping every fourth sample reduces the sample rate to 2000/4=500 Hz. | The new Nyquist interval ends at 250 Hz, so the original 300 Hz tone folds. | Its baseband alias is |300−500|=200 Hz.','A signal that was safe at the original rate may alias after decimation.',unit='Hz')
b.m(r'Using sinc(x)=sin(πx)/(πx), what is sinc(0) by continuous extension?','B',['0','1','π','Undefined even by limit'],'Normalized sinc; removable expression 0/0 at zero','Find the reconstructed interpolation kernel value at its origin.','Sinc interpolation kernel',r'\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin\pi x}{\pi x}=1',r'Use sin u/u→1 as u→0. | Set u=πx. | The continuous extension has sinc(0)=1, which allows exact interpolation of a sample at its own grid point.','The apparent 0/0 is removable rather than an infinite singularity.',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'For ideal uniform sampling of a baseband bandlimited signal, which statements are correct?',['A','B','D'],['The sampled spectrum contains shifted replicas spaced fs apart','Avoiding overlap requires a sufficient sampling rate relative to bandwidth','Sampling preserves all analog signals regardless of bandwidth','Ideal sinc reconstruction uses infinitely supported interpolation kernels'],'Ideal impulse sampling; ordinary baseband reconstruction','Select all valid spectral and interpolation statements.','Sampling spectrum and reconstruction',r'X_s(f)=f_s\sum_kX(f-kf_s)',r'Multiplication by an impulse train creates shifted spectral replicas at multiples of fs. | Adequate spacing separates their support; an arbitrary unlimited-bandwidth signal cannot meet that condition at finite fs. | Ideal low-pass reconstruction corresponds to an infinite-duration sinc kernel. Select A, B and D.','Finite-duration practical interpolators approximate the ideal sinc kernel.')
b.n(r'Two real tones at 600 Hz and 1400 Hz are sampled at 2000 Hz. How many distinct nonnegative baseband frequencies do their samples occupy?',1,'Two tones; fs=2000 Hz; phases arbitrary','Count distinct folded frequency locations.','Alias collision',r'f\sim|f-kf_s|',r'The 600 Hz tone already lies below fs/2=1000 Hz. | The 1400 Hz tone folds to |1400−2000|=600 Hz. | Both occupy one frequency location; their phases/amplitudes can combine but cannot make them separately identifiable from sampling alone.','Two analog tones can collapse to one sampled frequency.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'An analog signal contains tones only at 1 kHz and 4 kHz. A prefilter passes 1 kHz unchanged and completely rejects 4 kHz, followed by fs=3 ksample/s sampling. What is the highest tone frequency retained in the reconstructed baseband signal, in kHz?',1,'Ideal prefilter removes 4 kHz tone; 1 kHz passes; fs=3 kHz','Find the highest retained reconstructed tone.','Prefiltering before sampling',r'f_{pass,max}<f_s/2',r'After the analog filter, only the 1 kHz component remains. | The Nyquist interval extends to 1.5 kHz, so this passed tone does not alias. | Ideal reconstruction retains the 1 kHz tone. The rejected 4 kHz component contributes nothing.','Analyze the analog filter before computing aliases of the remaining spectrum.',difficulty='hard',unit='kHz')
